{"name":"Berry Syrup","hex":"#64537c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"},{"term":"syrup","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any thick liquid that has a high sugar content and which is added to or poured over food as a flavoring.","Any viscous liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To convert or process into syrup.","To add syrup to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/syrup"}],"entries":[{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"},{"title":"Syrup","blocks":[{"text":"In cooking, syrup is a thick, viscous, liquid condiment consisting primarily of a solution of sugar in water. It typically contains a large amount of dissolved sugars but shows little tendency to deposit crystals. In its concentrated form, its consistency is similar to that of molasses. The viscosity arises from the multiple hydrogen bonds between the dissolved sugar, which has many hydroxyl (OH) groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Maltose_syrup.jpg/330px-Maltose_syrup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrup","wikidata":"Q6584340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/500px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorwegian_blueberry.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/250px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/330px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG/250px-Ribes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibes_rubrum2005-07-17.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg/250px-Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg/330px-Lonicera_coerulea_a3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Lonicera_c